{#
	The plain-text half of the message an operator sends a stranded signup
	(XIV-108, §8.14).

	**This file is also the preview.** `SignupMailer::renderApology()` renders it
	and the tenant list draws the result beside the button, so what an operator
	reads before pressing send is the same string the recipient reads afterwards.
	Not a paraphrase of it, and not a second template that would drift from this
	one the first time a sentence was reworded. That is what makes "the
	operator sees what it says before it goes" a property rather than a promise.

	**No URL anywhere in it**, which is the difference from every other mail this
	application sends. There is nothing here to click: the installation does not
	exist, so there is no address to give, and the confirmation link this person
	already followed is spent. A message to somebody who is confused about why
	they have heard nothing, carrying a link, is the shape of the mail they should
	be suspicious of.

	The sentences are in the catalogue rather than here because they go out in the
	language the signup recorded (§8.14, as the invitation does), and a paragraph
	written into a template is a paragraph in one language.
#}
